description:
Add SDK support for intercepting and modifying HTTP requests/responses from VMs.

API design (TypeScript):
```typescript
const vm = await agentkernel.create({
  httpHooks: {
    onRequest: async (req) => {
      // Log, modify, or block requests
      console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
      return req; // or throw to block
    },
    onResponse: async (res, req) => {
      // Inspect responses
      return res;
    },
    allowedHosts: ["api.openai.com", "api.anthropic.com"],
  },
});
```

Use cases:
- Secret injection (see agentkernel-b7e)
- Request logging/auditing
- Rate limiting
- Cost tracking (count API calls)
- Block unauthorized endpoints
- Mock responses for testing

Implementation requires traffic interception layer (see agentkernel-b7e).

Implement secret injection where secrets never enter the VM. The host proxy intercepts HTTP requests and injects real credentials only for allowed hosts.

## The Pattern (Gondolin, Deno Sandbox)

```
Host Proxy                          Sandbox (VM)
┌─────────────────┐                ┌─────────────────┐
│ secrets: {      │                │ $OPENAI_API_KEY │
│   OPENAI_API_KEY│◄───request─────│ = placeholder   │
│   hosts: [api.  │                │                 │
│    openai.com]  │────inject──────► curl api.openai │
│ }               │    real key    │                 │
└─────────────────┘                └─────────────────┘
```

## MVP: HTTP Proxy Approach (like Deno)

1. Run proxy on host (e.g., 10.0.0.1:8080)
2. Set HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY in sandbox env
3. Proxy checks if request host is in allowedHosts
4. If secret is scoped to that host, inject into Authorization header
5. Forward request, return response

```rust
// Proxy logic
fn handle_request(req: Request, secrets: &SecretStore) -> Response {
    let host = req.uri().host();

    // Check allowlist
    if !secrets.is_host_allowed(host) {
        return Response::forbidden("Host not in allowlist");
    }

    // Inject secrets scoped to this host
    let mut req = req;
    for (name, secret) in secrets.for_host(host) {
        if req.headers().contains(&format!("Bearer {}", secret.placeholder)) {
            req.headers_mut().set(
                "Authorization", 
                format!("Bearer {}", secret.value)
            );
        }
    }

    forward(req)
}
```

## API Design

```bash
# CLI
agentkernel create my-sandbox \
  --secret OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-xxx:api.openai.com \
  --secret ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-xxx:api.anthropic.com \
  --allowed-hosts api.openai.com,api.anthropic.com

# Config file
[secrets]
OPENAI_API_KEY = { value = "sk-xxx", hosts = ["api.openai.com"] }

[network]
allowed_hosts = ["api.openai.com", "api.anthropic.com"]
```

## SDK (TypeScript)

```typescript
const sandbox = await agentkernel.create({
  secrets: {
    OPENAI_API_KEY: {
      value: process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY,
      hosts: ["api.openai.com"],
    },
  },
  allowedHosts: ["api.openai.com"],
});
```

## Security Benefits

- Secrets never visible inside VM (even with root access)
- Per-host scoping prevents exfiltration to evil.com
- Can log/audit all API calls
- Works with any language/tool in the VM

## Future: virtio-net Interception

For even stronger isolation (no proxy env vars needed), intercept at the network layer. But proxy is simpler and achieves same security goal.

